# Machine-specific Claude Code permissions. .claude/settings.json IS committed and shared.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Overleaf working area: the export zips and the clone of each Overleaf project. Never committed --
# the clone is a second git repository, and committing it would nest one repo inside another.
writing/exports/

# Material that is not ours to redistribute: co-authors' Word drafts and conversion scratch, kept
# beside the paper so they are findable, kept out of the history so the repo stays shareable.
writing/paper/localfiles/

# LaTeX compile artefacts
*.aux
*.bbl
*.blg
*.bcf
*.fdb_latexmk
*.fls
*.lof
*.log
*.lot
*.out
*.run.xml
*.synctex.gz
*.toc

# The compiled document, not a figure. `writing/*/main.pdf` rather than `*.pdf`, so that a figure
# committed under a project's Figs/ is kept -- the pipeline's outputs are sources here, not artefacts.
writing/*/main.pdf

# Julia: the Manifest IS committed, so a result can be reproduced on another machine. The model
# depends only on the standard library today, which is exactly when committing it costs nothing.

# Solved model output: regenerable from model/scripts/, and large. The summary csv that a table or a
# figure is built from IS committed when that pipeline exists -- decide per file, not by accident.
results/**/*.jld2
results/**/*.pkl

# The experiment output of model/scripts/run_experiments.jl: one CSV per experiment, rewritten by
# every run and regenerable from the calibration file. What survives a run is the generated table
# under writing/quant/Tables/, which IS committed.
model/output/

# Python (none in the repo today; the Overleaf script is stdlib-only and runs on any interpreter)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# B1: the Haas et al. (2020) supplement is committed as the zip it was downloaded as, and
# data/build/b1_haas2020.py unpacks the single workbook inside it on first run. The unpacked copy is
# a duplicate of two megabytes, not a second source.
data/raw/haas2020/Supporting Material Table S1 RCR.xlsx

# B2: Maddison and PWT each ship the same data twice, as xlsx and as Stata. The pipeline reads the
# xlsx; the .dta files stay on disk because they were downloaded, and out of the history because
# they are duplicates of a committed file.
data/raw/maddison2023/*.dta
data/raw/pwt1001/*.dta

# B2: the Piketty and Zucman (2014) "Capital is Back" workbooks carry no licence statement -- they
# are posted by the authors as the article's supporting data. Readable here, not ours to
# redistribute. data/sources/B2_macro.md carries the re-download command.
data/raw/piketty_zucman2014/

# B5: four open-access papers and assessment chapters, 44 MB between them, which are literature
# rather than data. Each data/build/b5_*.py downloads its own when it is absent, and
# data/sources/B5_pollution.md carries the commands. The Global Carbon Budget flat CSV, which is the
# block's actual data, IS committed.
data/raw/gcb/essd-18-3211-2026.pdf
data/raw/joos2013/
data/raw/ipcc_ar6_wg1/

# B3: the resource-side literature and the 17 MB Mineral Commodity Summaries report. They are
# sources to be transcribed from, not data to be read by a script: every number taken from them is
# a literal in data/build/b3_usgs_mcs.py, b3_oregrades.py, b3_urr.py or b3_exploration.py with its
# page or slide. Mudd (2010) is Elsevier's and the MinEx presentations are MinEx's, so neither is
# ours to redistribute. data/sources/B3_resources.md carries the download command for each.
# The Jacks workbook and the USGS Data Series 140 workbooks, which ARE read by a script, are
# committed.
data/raw/usgs_mcs/
data/raw/oregrades/
data/raw/urr/
data/raw/minex/
data/raw/ipcc2006/

# B4: the four report PDFs the block transcribes from and the seven-item recovery-cost literature,
# 48 MB between them. Every number taken from them is a literal in data/build/b4_whatawaste2.py,
# b4_unep_gwmo2024.py, b4_unep_irp2011.py, b4_circularity_gap.py or b4_xi_literature.py with its
# table and page; none is read by a script. Three of the seven literature items are
# all-rights-reserved and are not ours to redistribute. data/sources/B4_waste_recycling.md carries
# the download command for each. The What a Waste CSVs, the OECD dataflow and the Eurostat bulk
# files, which ARE read by a script, are committed.
data/raw/whatawaste2/*.pdf
data/raw/unep_gwmo2024/
data/raw/unep_irp2011/
data/raw/circularity_gap/
data/raw/xi_literature/
